-- Milestone 3 — families, guardians, children, and the transient registration
-- draft that carries a submission across a duplicate warning.
--
-- The section above the "HAND-WRITTEN" banner is generated by Prisma.
-- Everything below it is reviewed by a human, because Prisma cannot express
-- CHECK constraints, partial indexes, sequences, functions or triggers. Each
-- hand-written object is named, and an integration test asserts every name
-- exists in the PostgreSQL catalogue, so a future edit cannot quietly drop one.
--
-- This migration is CREATE-ONLY WITH ONE EXCEPTION: it adds four enums, four
-- tables, and one CHECK constraint to the existing `audit_log`. That one
-- `ALTER TABLE` validates every existing row as it is applied. A read-only scan
-- of the development database before writing this confirmed the compatibility
-- it depends on: the audit payloads written by Milestones 1 and 2 use eleven
-- distinct keys, none of which normalises to `nationalid`, `mobile`, `landline`
-- or `phone`, so validation is instantaneous and cannot fail. Nothing else that
-- Milestone 1 or 2 created is altered.
--
-- ON TIMESTAMPS: as in both earlier migrations, `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` is
-- PostgreSQL's exact synonym for `transaction_timestamp()` — the transaction
-- start time, not the wall clock at statement execution. Every row written in
-- one transaction shares an instant, so a state change and its audit row are
-- provably simultaneous. The spelling is left as Prisma generates it so
-- schema-drift detection cannot produce a false positive on a synonym.
--
-- ON THE SEQUENCE: `child_file_number_seq` is deliberately NOT attached as a
-- column default. Prisma sees column defaults, so an attached sequence would
-- have to be described in the datamodel or read as permanent drift; the service
-- calls `nextval()` explicitly in its INSERT instead.

